nd":"person","storyRole":"supporting","roleVisibleAt":{"chapterNumber":7,"paragraphIndex":40,"offset":0},"firstMention":{"chapterNumber":7,"paragraphIndex":40,"offset":0},"snapshots":[{"availableAt":{"chapterNumber":7,"paragraphIndex":40,"offset":0},"name":"Sarah","subtitle":"Abraham's wife, kept in the dark","body":"Abraham's wife, whom Problema III examines through the question of whether it was ethically defensible that Abraham concealed his undertaking from her entirely — one of the three, alongside Eliezer and Isaac, that Abraham passes by in silence."}]},{"id":"eliezer","kind":"person","storyRole":"supporting","roleVisibleAt":{"chapterNumber":7,"paragraphIndex":40,"offset":0},"firstMention":{"chapterNumber":7,"paragraphIndex":40,"offset":0},"snapshots":[{"availableAt":{"chapterNumber":7,"paragraphIndex":40,"offset":0},"name":"Eliezer","subtitle":"Abraham's servant, also unknowing","body":"Abraham's servant, named alongside Sarah and Isaac as one of the three ethical claims on Abraham that his silence passes by — the ordinary bonds of household and family that an ethical man would be expected to explain himself to, and that Abraham does not."}]},{"id":"johannes-de-silentio","kind":"person","storyRole":"central","roleVisibleAt":{"chapterNumber":1,"paragraphIndex":3,"offset":0},"firstMention":{"chapterNumber":1,"paragraphIndex":3,"offset":0},"snapshots":[{"availableAt":{"chapterNumber":1,"paragraphIndex":3,"offset":0},"name":"Johannes de Silentio","subtitle":"The pseudonymous author","body":"The pseudonym under which the entire book is signed — \"Most respectfully, Johannes de Silentio\" — a fictional author who repeatedly insists he does not himself have faith, and writes as an admirer straining to understand a movement of soul he cannot make himself."}]},{"id":"hegel","kind":"person","storyRole":"supporting","roleVisibleAt":{"chapterNumber":4,"paragraphIndex":9,"offset":0},"firstMention":{"chapterNumber":4,"paragraphIndex":9,"offset":0},"snapshots":[{"availableAt":{"chapterNumber":4,"paragraphIndex":9,"offset":0},"name":"Hegel","subtitle":"The philosophy Johannes writes against","body":"The philosopher named as the target of the \"Preliminary Expostulation\": it is said to be hard to understand Hegel, Johannes remarks, but to understand Abraham a small matter — a claim he sets out to overturn by making Abraham the harder case."}]},{"id":"knight-of-infinite-resignation","kind":"personification","storyRole":"major","roleVisibleAt":{"chapterNumber":4,"paragraphIndex":13,"offset":0},"firstMention":{"chapterNumber":4,"paragraphIndex":13,"offset":0},"snapshots":[{"availableAt":{"chapterNumber":4,"paragraphIndex":13,"offset":0},"name":"The Knight of Infinite Resignation","subtitle":"Who gives up the finite for the eternal","body":"The figure who has made the last purely human movement of the soul: renouncing a finite desire entirely and finding peace and rest in that renunciation alone — a stage Johannes says he can himself understand and even in some sense achieve, unlike what comes after it."}]},{"id":"knight-of-faith","kind":"personification","storyRole":"central","roleVisibleAt":{"chapterNumber":4,"paragraphIndex":16,"offset":0},"firstMention":{"chapterNumber":4,"paragraphIndex":16,"offset":0},"snapshots":[{"availableAt":{"chapterNumber":4,"paragraphIndex":16,"offset":0},"name":"The Knight of Faith","subtitle":"Fear and Trembling's true subject","body":"The figure who, unlike the knight of resignation, gets the finite back after giving it up — believing by virtue of the absurd that what has been renounced will nonetheless be restored. Johannes says he would walk on foot to meet such a person, and confesses he has never found one."}]},{"id":"the-absurd","kind":"personification","storyRole":"central","roleVisibleAt":{"chapterNumber":4,"paragraphIndex":12,"offset":0},"firstMention":{"chapterNumber":4,"paragraphIndex":12,"offset":0},"snapshots":[{"availableAt":{"chapterNumber":4,"paragraphIndex":12,"offset":0},"name":"The Absurd","subtitle":"Faith's paradoxical object","body":"What faith, on Johannes's account, actually believes by virtue of: not despite reason but past the point where human calculation can follow — that Abraham would get Isaac back in this life, against all worldly likelihood, simply because God who demanded him was the same God who had promised him."}]},{"id":"teleological-suspension-of-the-ethical","kind":"personification","storyRole":"central","roleVisibleAt":{"chapterNumber":5,"paragraphIndex":6,"offset":0},"firstMention":{"chapterNumber":5,"paragraphIndex":6,"offset":0},"snapshots":[{"availableAt":{"chapterNumber":5,"paragraphIndex":6,"offset":0},"name":"The Teleological Suspension of the Ethical","subtitle":"Problema I's question","body":"The idea Problema I puts directly: whether the single individual can stand higher than the universal ethical order for the sake of a higher, absolute relation to God — the concept Johannes argues Abraham's story requires, and which places him fundamentally outside the ethical as tragic heroes are not."}]}],"mentions":[{"characterId":"abraham","chapterNumber":3,"paragraphIndex":1,"startOffset":1506,"endOffset":1539,"text":"Abraham was greater than everyone"},{"characterId":"isaac","chapterNumber":3,"paragraphIndex":5,"startOffset":411,"endOffset":421,"text":"Take Isaac"},{"characterId":"sarah","chapterNumber":7,"paragraphIndex":40,"startOffset":43,"endOffset":51,"text":"to Sarah"},{"characterId":"eliezer","chapterNumber":7,"paragraphIndex":40,"startOffset":57,"endOffset":67,"text":"to Eliezer"},{"characterId":"johannes-de-silentio","chapterNumber":1,"paragraphIndex":3,"startOffset":19,"endOffset":39,"text":"Johannes de silentio"},{"characterId":"hegel","chapterNumber":4,"paragraphIndex":9,"startOffset":329,"endOffset":348,"text":"Understanding Hegel"},{"characterId":"knight-of-infinite-resignation","chapterNumber":4,"paragraphIndex":13,"startOffset":81,"endOffset":101,"text":"infinite resignation"},{"characterId":"knight-of-faith","chapterNumber":4,"paragraphIndex":16,"startOffset":559,"endOffset":574,"text":"knight of faith"},{"characterId":"the-absurd","chapterNumber":4,"paragraphIndex":12,"startOffset":298,"endOffset":308,"text":"the absurd"},{"characterId":"teleological-suspension-of-the-ethical","chapterNumber":5,"paragraphIndex":6,"startOffset":47,"endOffset":85,"text":"teleological suspension of the ethical"}]}}}